        "retirement_plan.scenarios[2].projected_at_retirement_usd",
        "retirement_plan.scenarios[2].net_return_pct",
        "assumptions.withdrawal_rate_pct",
        "assumptions.pre_tax_accounts_reduced_by_pct"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Historical 2008 drawdown",
      "body": "In a replay of the 2008–09 financial crisis, the portfolio fell 56.4%, or $8,460, and took 24.6 months to recover. The replay holds today’s weights constant and uses proxies where history is missing; it describes that past period, not a forecast.",
      "fact_keys": [
        "crash_replays[0].max_drawdown_usd",
        "crash_replays[0].max_drawdown_pct",
        "crash_replays[0].months_to_recover",
        "assumptions.crash_replay_method"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Two large company positions",
      "body": "NVIDIA is 14.3% of the portfolio and Apple is 13.7%, so both exceed the 10% concentration threshold. The look-through estimate covers 44.6% of invested assets; company exposure through funds may be higher because fund holdings are reported only for top positions.",
      "fact_keys": [
        "concentration.companies_over_threshold[0].pct",
        "concentration.companies_over_threshold[1].pct",
        "assumptions.concentration_threshold_pct",
        "look_through.coverage_pct_of_invested",
        "look_through.note"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "advisor_questions": [
    "How should I interpret the range of retirement projections given the return, withdrawal, and tax assumptions?",
    "How would a drawdown like the 2008–09 replay relate to my risk tolerance and time horizon?",
    "How much of my exposure to NVIDIA and Apple comes through funds as well as direct holdings?"
  ]
